    "body": "## Motivation\n\nWith `--enable-streaming-session` and `--schedule-policy dfs-weight`, the scheduler crashes as soon as a later streaming-session turn waits in the queue.\n\nThat turn's prefix match returns its session slot's virtual node (`StreamingSession.try_match_prefix`), which has no path in the tree. `SchedulePolicy._sort_by_dfs_weight` hands every waiting request's `last_node` to `UnifiedRadixCache.dfs_weight_order`, and the tree core cannot resolve the virtual node:\n\n- Python tree core: `KeyError: <..._VirtualNode object at ...>`\n- Rust tree core: `TypeError: '_VirtualNode' object cannot be interpreted as an integer`\n\nBoth raise inside `get_new_batch_prefill`, so the whole scheduler goes down. Locking already treats the virtual node as a special case (`UnifiedRadixCache.lock`), but the dfs-weight ordering does not.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- `UnifiedRadixCache.dfs_weight_order` replaces a virtual node with the root handle before calling the tree core, so a session turn is ordered with the requests that match nothing in the tree. Its KV lives in the session slot, not in a subtree the sort could group it with.\n- New test `TestStreamingSessionLockLifecycle.test_dfs_weight_policy_orders_a_waiting_session_turn`. It builds a real later turn whose `last_node` is the slot's virtual node, puts it in a waiting queue with a plain request, runs `SchedulePolicy(\"dfs-weight\", ...).calc_priority`, and finishes with `sanity_check`.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nNot applicable. No model-side change.\n\n## Benchmarking and Profiling\n\nNot applicable.\n\nHow I tested: CPU only, no model. The test file is registered for CUDA CI, so to run it locally I patched the cache fixtures' `get_device` to return `\"cpu\"`. On `main` (efc9849520) the new test fails with `KeyError: <sglang.srt.session.streaming_session._VirtualNode ...>`; with this change all 6 tests in `TestStreamingSessionLockLifecycle` pass.\n\nI found this with a CPU scheduler driver that runs the real `Scheduler` with streaming sessions and random scheduling policies. With `dfs-weight`, it hit the Rust-core `TypeError` above in `get_next_batch_to_run`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit). I ran isort 7.0.0, ruff 0.15.1 (format, plus the pre-commit `--select=F401,F821,F823,UP037` check) and codespell 2.4.1 on the changed files.\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations). Not needed.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #40077251768](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40077251768)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #40077251870](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40077251870)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #40077251894](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40077251894)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
